<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">John I of Portugal</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1357">1357</a>–<a href="/years/1433">1433</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as King João I · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-aviz">House of Aviz</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">John I of Portugal was the founder of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-aviz">House of Aviz</a>, who secured the throne after a succession crisis in the late fourteenth century. His reign confirmed Portuguese independence from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a>. In 1415 his forces captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ceuta">Ceuta</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a>, often regarded as the start of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/portuguese-empire">Portuguese Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/john-i-of-portugal.jpg" alt="John I of Portugal" width="320" height="417" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: anonymous · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Anoniem_-_Koning_Johan_I_van_Portugal_(1450-1500)_-_Lissabon_Museu_Nacional_de_Arte_Antiga_19-10-2010_16-12-61.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>During the crisis of 1383–1385 John, <b>Master of Avis</b>, and his constable Nuno Álvares Pereira defeated a much larger Castilian army at the Battle of Aljubarrota on 14 August 1385, with the help of English archers, and the Treaty of Windsor of 1386 began an alliance with England…</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-portugal">Kingdom of Portugal · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>…15th century brought a financial crisis that ended the subsidies to the marabouts and sharifian families, and the Christian powers struck the coast: Henry III of Castile seized Tetouan in 1399, massacred half its people and enslaved the rest, and <b>John I of Portugal</b> took Ceuta in 1415.</blockquote><a href="/empire/marinid-sultanate">Marinid Sultanate · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>…conquest of the Muslim port of Ceuta on the North African coast on 21 August 1415, led by <b>King João I</b> and his sons, including Prince Henry, later celebrated as “the Navigator”, began the imperial project, though Ceuta itself proved a commercial disappointment as Saharan caravan trade bypassed…</blockquote><a href="/empire/portuguese-empire">Portuguese Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The European colonial age is conventionally dated from 1415, when the army of <b>John I of Portugal</b> and his sons, among them Prince Henry the Navigator, took the Muslim port of Ceuta on the Moroccan coast.</blockquote><a href="/government/colonial-rule">Colonial rule · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>The army of <b>John I of Portugal</b> and his constable Nuno Álvares Pereira, with English allies, decisively defeated the larger army of John I of Castile.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/august-14">14 August in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lisbon">Lisbon</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/portugal">Portugal</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lisbon">Lisbon</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/portugal">Portugal</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=38.71,-9.14&amp;year=1406" aria-label="Lisbon on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4323 775 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of John I of Portugal"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4773 1045h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4773 1045h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=38.71,-9.14&amp;year=1406">What was here in 1406? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
